# OSPF Multi-Router Network Configuration Using Cisco Packet Tracer

## 1. Project Overview

This project demonstrates the design, configuration, and verification of a multi-router network using **Open Shortest Path First (OSPF)** in Cisco Packet Tracer.

The network consists of four interconnected routers (R1, R2, R3, and R4) configured with multiple interface types, including GigabitEthernet, FastEthernet, and Serial connections.

The project focuses on implementing dynamic routing with OSPF Area 0, configuring Layer 2 switchports using VLAN interfaces, assigning unique OSPF Router IDs, advertising a Loopback network, and verifying routing connectivity between all routers.

### Project Objectives

- Design a four-router network topology.
- Configure IPv4 addresses on router interfaces.
- Configure GigabitEthernet and Serial interfaces.
- Configure FastEthernet switchports using VLANs.
- Implement single-area OSPF routing.
- Assign unique OSPF Router IDs.
- Configure and advertise a Loopback network.
- Understand OSPF Administrative Distance and Cost.
- Verify OSPF neighbor relationships.
- Examine OSPF-learned routes in the routing table.
- Test network connectivity using ICMP Ping.
- Troubleshoot interface and routing problems.

## 2. Network Topology

The topology consists of four routers connected in a redundant network.

![OSPF Network Topology](images/ospf-topology.png)

*Figure 1: Four-router OSPF topology created in Cisco Packet Tracer.*

### Network Structure

```text
                     R2
                   /    \
        10.10.12.0/24   10.10.24.0/24
                 /        \
                R1--------R4
                 \ Serial /|
                  \      / | Loopback0
                   \    /  | 192.168.1.1/24
                    R3     |
```

The logical connections are:

| Connection | Network | Interface Types |
|---|---|---|
| R1 ↔ R2 | 10.10.12.0/24 | FastEthernet |
| R1 ↔ R3 | 10.10.13.0/24 | GigabitEthernet |
| R1 ↔ R4 | 10.10.14.0/24 | Serial |
| R2 ↔ R4 | 10.10.24.0/24 | FastEthernet |
| R3 ↔ R4 | 10.10.34.0/24 | GigabitEthernet |
| R4 Loopback | 192.168.1.0/24 | Loopback0 |

All four routers participate in OSPF Area 0.

The redundant connections allow OSPF to calculate alternative paths between routers.

